      "writtenOn": "2026-04-23T19:30:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point. Actually perhaps this race is not extremely unlikely to occur in practice, since the host may be logging to dmesg quite frequently, thus also writing to ramoops quite frequency?\n\nSo we might still want to address this, to avoid ramoops corruption in such case, even if it is not a security issue?\n\nSo, we might still want to use pkvm_kick_vcpu() + pkvm_wait_vcpu_kicked_out() for all CPUs instead of broadcast INIT? I\u0027m still wondering if there are any advantages of broadcast INIT here (I asked about that in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/4032091/comment/58194db0_f6b36d5f/).\n\nWell, maybe the advantage is: broadcast INIT is simpler and possibly more reliable, in particular, `pkvm_kick_vcpu()` only kicks the vCPU if its vcpu-\u003emode is IN_GUEST_MODE, while in our case here (i.e. before panic) pKVM is in some buggy undefined state, so _theoretically_ vcpu-\u003emode may have an incorrect value...\n\nIf that is a concern, we might do broadcast INIT + pkvm_wait_vcpu_kicked_out() instead?",
